The text-UI library, the first port of roadmap block 3 (DESIGN D27 sections 8 and 9; docs/d27-c-library.md,
"The text-UI library on Zenix")

What it is: the owner's tui.library, the toolkit of Sumi, Maki and Markdown on AmigaOS (a grid of cells drawn with
escape sequences, only what changed sent; colours, attributes, box lines, keys and their escape codes). On Zenix it
is linked into each program that uses it ($(BUILD)/libtui.a, D27 8: no shared library), over the C library.

upstream/  the owner's source, one source for AmigaOS and Zenix (D27 9, rule 1; owner 2026-10-28), pinned here and
           never changed: the owner's AmigaLibs repository (public, MIT), commit
           3b74efdd97886112c2b8808d8e8e4d4323a85134 (2026-10-22), tui.library 0.5b1: src/core.c (the toolkit),
           src/glue.c (the old tui_* names), src/version.h, include/ (libraries/tui.h, clib/tui_protos.h, tui.h)
           and LICENSE (MIT, the notice that travels with any program built from it: tools/mkroot.sh puts it in
           /usr/share/licenses/tui next to /tests/tuitest).
zenix/     Zenix's layer (GPL-2.0-only), kept apart so the copy can be refreshed:
           core.c     compiles upstream/src/core.c unchanged and gives its terminal half on Zenix: termios raw mode
                      (cfmakeraw: ^C and ^Z are keys), poll for every wait, TIOCGWINSZ for the size, a BPTR given
                      to TuiOpen/TuiOpenIO/TuiPossible is a handle, the descriptor + 1 (ports/common's handles; 0 is
                      descriptor 0 for the input, 1 for the output); the upstream terminal functions are renamed by #define and declared static
                      first, so they are compiled but never emitted (the list and the reasons: the file's head)
           glue.c     upstream/src/glue.c as its TUI_STATIC build, tui_possible answered by TuiPossible
           sigfd.c    tui_zenix_sigfd, the descriptor behind a signal bit for TuiWait's extra signals: none here, a
                      member of the library of its own, so a program's own (ports/common/dos.c: its reply ports, a
                      pipe Maki reads) is linked instead when it has one
           include/   amiga.h, the AmigaOS types and the few calls the drawing half makes (Write, AllocVec, FreeVec,
                      CopyMem, CurrentTime) over the C library; the calls only the replaced terminal half makes are
                      declared and defined nowhere (a link error if a kept part ever starts to use one); the
                      exec/, dos/ and proto/ headers the upstream file includes are amiga.h

On Zenix the toolkit's public fields say: CTerm 1 (the console takes the ANSI colours as they are, 256 and 24-bit
ones as the nearest of its 16, and ESC [ ? 25 h/l for the cursor: upstream's CTerm path), UTF8 0, CellW and CellH 0.
No mouse, no pastes, no resize events (the console never changes size), no second screen (closing clears it).
TuiSignal answers 0: a program with a poll loop of its own polls descriptor 0 and calls TuiPoll. TuiWait's extra
signals are descriptors (tui_zenix_sigfd names the one behind each bit): the wait ends with TE_NONE and the bit in
*got when it has data, as upstream's extra signals end it.

Widths and UTF-8: the console is Latin-1 today (one byte one cell), so the toolkit writes Latin-1 and the stand-ins
upstream gives a plain console (box lines as - | +, Lodz for the Polish name, quotes and dashes as ' " -); widths
are upstream's own table (CTerm's, without utf8.library). The switch TUI_UTF8=1 (make TUI_UTF8=1, the define
ZENIX_TUI_UTF8) turns on UTF-8 output and the C library's wcwidth (through upstream's own TUI_LIB way to
utf8.library's widths, include/proto/utf8.h; composing accents onto letters is not there): off until the console's
UTF-8 (DESIGN D28) is built. Every build compiles it ($(BUILD)/tui/core-utf8.o, not linked) so it keeps building.

Found in upstream (for the owner's repository, not changed here): a 24-bit colour whose green value has bit 1 set
(TC_RGB(r, g, b) with g = 2, 3, 6, 7, ... 255) is taken for a pen colour (TC_PEN, bit 0x200) by sgrcol and to16any,
which test 0x200 before 0x1000000: it is drawn as pen g & 7 instead. tuitest uses a red that avoids it.

Tests: tools/tui-test.sh (FS-UAE, the stock A1200; in make check): /tests/tuitest (user/tuitest.c) draws its widgets
and reads the console's grid back.

To update: tools/tui-vendor.sh /path/to/AmigaLibs [COMMIT] (the files above from that commit, unchanged), put the
commit here, build, run tools/tui-test.sh; if upstream's terminal half gained a function, add it to zenix/core.c's
list (the link fails if a kept part calls an AmigaOS function amiga.h only declares).
